// Which sub-expression speaks first when both are wrong: the callee or the
// argument.
//
// `b.nomethod(b.nofield)` has neither the method nor the field, so the only
// question is which one the compiler reports. The reference elaborates the
// arguments on its way to the call and reports the ARGUMENT; the port answered
// about the missing method and the argument's own error never surfaced at all.
//
// The shape has nothing to do with the prelude, and that is why it is here
// rather than in a shadowing fixture: it was FOUND through three prelude cells
// where the reference named a field and the port named a method, and it reduces
// to five lines with no prelude name in them.
struct Box { a: int }

fn main() {
    let b = Box { a: 1 };
    b.nomethod(b.nofield);
}
